 ## Overview
-Transient 2D Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) estimates the radiance contribution of photon beams to a camera ray in a homogeneous participating medium by projecting the interaction onto a 2D spatial kernel and a 1D temporal window. It solves the problem of computing time-resolved light transport (transient rendering) efficiently by approximating the continuous beam contribution as a discrete density estimate. It differs from the standard 3D KDE [Kernel Density Estimation for Volume Reconstruction](9516-kernel-density-estimation-for-volume-reconstruction.md) by explicitly modeling the temporal footprint of the light pulse, and from the 1D kernel variant by distributing energy over a temporal interval rather than a single instant, introducing a trade-off between temporal bias and computational simplicity.
+Transient 2D Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) estimates the radiance contribution of photon beams to a camera ray in a homogeneous participating medium by projecting the interaction onto a 2D spatial kernel and a 1D temporal window [S1]. It solves the problem of computing time-resolved light transport (transient rendering) efficiently by approximating the continuous beam contribution as a discrete density estimate [S1]. It differs from the standard 3D KDE [Kernel Density Estimation for Volume Reconstruction](9516-kernel-density-estimation-for-volume-reconstruction.md) by explicitly modeling the temporal footprint of the light pulse, and from the 1D kernel variant by distributing energy over a temporal interval rather than a single instant, introducing a trade-off between temporal bias and computational simplicity [S1].
 
